' LEAPING LARRY
' The action game by Michael Streeter from COMPUTE! magazine, December
' 1987 (Commodore 64; Apple II version by Bill Chin, Atari version by
' Rhett Anderson), for the ColecoVision in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Larry, an apprentice inventor, left his plans in a factory guarded by
' robots, with the elevators shut down. From the bottom floor he must leap
' from conveyor belt to conveyor belt up six floors to the plans on the top
' floor. The belts move a little faster than Larry walks: standing still,
' the belt slides out from under him and he falls. Levels 1-6: the belts
' get shorter. From level 7 one more floor is invisible each level.
' Touching a robot costs a Larry; there are three.
'
' Scoring (as the Commodore 64 version): top floor plans 600, bottom floor
' plans 100, falling plans 50.
'
' Controls (either joystick): the stick walks; a side button leaps, but
' only while walking. Keypad not used.
'
' Liberties taken:
' - The belts' lengths, speeds and directions were chosen for this version
'   (the listing is machine code); the belts slide smoothly, pixel by pixel.
' - The falling plans drift down the screen from time to time; catch them.
' - A falling Larry lands on the next belt or floor below him (no harm).
' - Larry can leap on the top floor and the ground too, to hop over a robot.
' - A best score is kept until power off. New music and sound effects.
'
' House rules kept: columns 2-29, rows 1-22; Larry (2 sprites) and a robot
' (2) share a floor, at most four sprites on a line except for a moment
' when the falling plans pass; the frame loop is protected from the NMI
' and every screen is drawn with the NMI off after a wait.

const TBelt0e = 128
const TBelt0l1 = 129
const TBelt0l2 = 130
